From 588b8837af21450955a806f9d73c52a4f1938074 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 21:48:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09109/11362] Refuse to measure a flag the benchmark cannot
 see: 47 of 95 would have filed as duds

Extends REQ-ARC-FLAG-LEDGER-6268, found by asking what happens the first time the
ledger measures something rather than assuming it works.

`arc_bench.py` drives `graph_explore_solve_v2`, not the full `E3AgentPolicy`
cascade. Computed by transitive import closure: 48 of the 95 tracked flags are
inside that closure and 47 are not.

Setting an unreachable flag and running the sweep returns a byte-identical result,
because the code that reads it never executes. The promotion rule would then
record HOLD -- "no level gained and no clear efficiency gain" -- which reads as
"this capability is worthless." That verdict would be wrong for 47 flags, and
wrong in the worst available direction: a ledger that systematically files real
capabilities as duds, with evidence attached, at a rate of one per measurement.
That is worse than the no-selection state this ledger was built to fix, because
it looks like data.

So `--measure` now refuses an unreachable flag and says why. `--status` reports
the split. `--force` still records the null, but it has to be typed, and a forced
entry is stamped `benchmark_reachable: false` so a later reader cannot mistake it
for evidence the capability does nothing. Refusing to measure is a smaller error
than measuring the wrong thing confidently.

The closure walks both `ast` imports and function-level `from carnot.agentic.X
import` -- this codebase imports inside functions constantly, and a missed edge
understates reachability, which is the exact error this function exists to
prevent.

Also fixes a real bug the new tests found: `cmd_measure` ended with
`LEDGER.relative_to(REPO)`, which raises when the ledger is not under the repo.
An exception there fires AFTER an eleven-minute measurement has been saved,
turning a completed run into a traceback for the sake of a shorter filename.

23 tests.

